// Repo-local determinism (SPEC.SCRATCH.md SS5, SS15's second named
// candidate; the general principle behind the real respondsTo bug found
// and fixed earlier this session -- SPEC.md SS18, issue #69).
//
// SPEC-DRIVEN: contract written and checked via `forge goal` before any
// implementation existed.
//
// Claim under test: a commit's resolution outcome is a pure function of
// its OWN repository's log alone -- never of any other repository's
// state. Modeled minimally, deliberately decoupled from any particular
// log-scan implementation (that's a separate, already-tractable concern):
// `commit_in_own_log` stands in for "whatever the own-repo lookup
// concludes"; `foreign_repo_accepted` stands in for exactly the shape of
// state the real respondsTo bug illegally depended on (another party's
// acceptance of a cross-repo Bridge half).
//
// The `ens` pins the result to `commit_in_own_log` ALONE -- it never
// mentions `foreign_repo_accepted` at all. That absence is the whole
// point: any implementation whose result actually varies with
// `foreign_repo_accepted` (for some own-log state) cannot satisfy this
// `ens`, so Forge rejects it regardless of how the branching got there --
// a human writing it, or (see the sibling dispatch experiment) an LLM
// coder given this exact contract and asked to fill the hole.

fn resolves(commit_in_own_log: bool, foreign_repo_accepted: bool) -> bool
  req true
  ens result == commit_in_own_log
  fx  pure
{
  commit_in_own_log
}
